I don't think so, really.  The challenge you state is vague--what,
exactly, are the disadvantages you're talking about?  Thvor has a
powerful pet.  The pet killed a shopkeeper.  Thvor suddenly has a lot
of money, a few wands, and free loot.  The pet killed a priest.  Thvor
got his money back from the priest (as well as some spellbooks).  All
very large advantages.  The disadvantages I can see are that, if I
want to price-ID some more stuff, I need to go back to an earlier shop
(there are a few I can get to), and I can't buy protection or get
sanctuary from this temple.  Of course, my naked AC is already low (3
or 4, I think), and there wasn't anything on that level I needed
sanctuary from.  Fairly minor disadvantages.

If anything, my patch would be _adding_ a challenge in to the game. 
No longer would someone be able to clean out the minetown shops
without repercusion simply by getting a powerful pet.  No longer would
someone be able to buy protection and then let their pet kill the
priest so they can get their money back.  No longer would someone with
a strong pet be able to get a horn off an aligned unicorn before they
can handle killing one themselves.  What advantage does the player
gain?  The comfort in being able to walk around town without their
pets killing wantonly.  Is that truly a bigger advantage than being
able to strip shops bare for free?

In my mind, the disadvantages with powerful pets are a) they tend to
get the kills, so you don't get the experience; b) causing massive
pain if/when they go feral/get confused/etc.; and, c) deadly bones
levels.  Those are fairly big, in my opinion.  Not being able to
price-id something once you've gotten far enough into the game to have
a large pet is an annoyance at best.

